    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hinges... that sounds nice, but i think the whole structure is rotten. every time i try to make a \"tiny change,\" the weight of everything just pushes back harder. it's like i'm fighting gravity. if you're at the bottom of a well you dug yourself, you don't just \"rearrange\" the walls. you're just stuck there. i look at the walls and i don't see a way to fix them or walk through them. i just see them as the end of the line. there's no version of this where i'm happy again. it's just a cycle of trying and failing.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "That image of the well is so vivid, and it perfectly captures the feeling of being overwhelmed by the sheer scale of the problem. When the structure feels completely rotten, the idea of small fixes can seem like a cruel joke. If the exit isn't visible, maybe the immediate goal shouldn't be \"happiness,\" but simply finding a way to make the pressure *less* intense right now. What is the smallest, most immediate point of relief you could allow yourself, even if it's just for five minutes?",
      "source": "target",
